2026-08-13T15:49:13.100922Z  INFO session_loop{thread_id=019ffbb6-7fc2-7180-9886-d34c68354dde}:submission_dispatch{otel.name="op.dispatch.user_input" submission.id="019ffbcc-09b3-7e70-8ca4-8a0ff5ee3455" codex.op="user_input"}:turn{otel.name="session_task.turn" thread.id=019ffbb6-7fc2-7180-9886-d34c68354dde turn.id=019ffbcc-09b3-7e70-8ca4-8a0ff5ee3455 model=gpt-5.6-sol codex.turn.reasoning_effort=medium}: codex_core::stream_events_utils: ToolCall: exec const patch = "*** Begin Patch\n*** Delete File: specs/006-machine-discovery-and-selection/plan.md\n*** Add File: specs/006-machine-discovery-and-selection/plan.md\n+# Implementation Plan: Machine Discovery and Selection\n+\n+**Feature**: `006-machine-discovery-and-selection`  \n+**Branch**: `machine-discovery-and-selection-feature`  \n+**Specification**: `specs/006-machine-discovery-and-selection/spec.md`  \n+**Date**: 2026-08-13\n+\n+## Summary\n+\n+Build a standalone, authenticated consumer machine-discovery boundary owned by\n+the new application. NestJS and PostgreSQL own the customer catalog, exact\n+identifier mappings, price, publication state, and availability evidence. The\n+API projects a strict allowlisted representation for Expo/React Native; mobile\n+never receives raw telemetry or internal identifiers and never talks to MQTT or\n+ERP.\n+\n+The first implementation uses controlled fixtures and an application-owned\n+availability emulator behind a replaceable port. ERP reference material is only\n+a read-only example of domain behavior. There is no current or future ERP\n+integration, adapter, client, import, synchronization, polling, webhook, shared\n+database, or runtime dependency.\n+\n+## Technical Context\n+\n+**Language/Version**: TypeScript 5.9, Node.js 22, React 19.1  \n+**Backend**: NestJS 11, Prisma 6, PostgreSQL 18, RxJS 7  \n+**Mobile**: Expo SDK 54, React Native 0.81.5, MobX 6, RxJS 7, React Navigation 7  \n+**New mobile runtime dependencies**: `expo-location`, `expo-camera`,\n+`react-native-maps`, installed with Expo's SDK-aware installer  \n+**Storage**: Application-owned PostgreSQL; Redis only for existing platform\n+concerns, not as the catalog authority  \n+**Testing**: Vitest, Supertest, Testcontainers, React Native Testing Library,\n+Maestro  \n+**Target platforms**: NestJS API, iOS, Android  \n+**Performance goals**: first 20 catalog entries within 2 seconds under the\n+specified reference conditions; detail/identifier responses within 3 seconds;\n+stable bounded pagination  \n+**Security**: authenticated endpoints, validated inputs, safe error taxonomy,\n+allowlisted DTOs, no raw telemetry/topics/credentials/internal identifiers  \n+**Scale assumption**: MVP regional catalog; no evidence presently justifies\n+PostGIS or a separate search service\n+\n+## Constitution Check\n+\n+| Principle | Plan evidence | Status |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| Protect user value | Selection is read-only and cannot debit, reserve, purchase, dispense, or command a machine. | PASS |\n+| Explicit state and transitions | Publication, operational evidence, projected availability, request generations, scanner state, and selection handoff are typed. | PASS |\n+| Secure boundaries | Auth guard, validation DTOs, safe errors, opaque IDs, exact code lookup, and allowlisted responses are mandatory. | PASS |\n+| Observable workflows | Mobile adapters are Observable-first; workflows use cancellation/supersession and session epochs. | PASS |\n+| Thin UI and centralized logic | Controllers and screens are presentation-oriented; services/stores own business and reactive logic. | PASS |\n+| Forward schema evolution | All new tables, enums, constraints, and indexes are introduced by reviewed forward migrations. | PASS |\n+| Deterministic behavior | Distance sorting, `machineId` tie-breaks, revision-bound cursors, exact aliases, fixture clock, and emulator scenarios are specified. | PASS |\n+\n+No constitution violation is required. Re-check after design: the Phase 1 model\n+and contracts retain these properties and introduce no exception.\n+\n+## Source Boundaries\n+\n+### Authoritative inputs\n+\n+1. `specs/006-machine-discovery-and-selection/spec.md`\n+2. Relevant discovery and machine-selection sections of\n+   `docs/mvp-requirements.md`\n+3. Current repository architecture and constitution\n+4. Mockups 03, 04, and 10 for visual hierarchy only\n+\n+`docs/integrations/machine-discovery-compatibility-report.md` and\n+`../erp-reference` are non-authoritative examples used only to understand domain\n+behavior, legacy ambiguity, and disclosure risks. Recommendations that imply ERP\n+ownership or integration are superseded by this plan.\n+\n+## Project Structure\n+\n+### Planning artifacts\n+\n+```text\n+specs/006-machine-discovery-and-selection/\n+├── plan.md\n+├── research.md\n+├── data-model.md\n+├── quickstart.md\n+├── contracts/\n+│   ├── machine-discovery.openapi.yaml\n+│   └── selection-handoff.md\n+└── spec.md\n+```\n+\n+### Backend boundaries\n+\n+```text\n+apps/api/\n+├── prisma/\n+│   ├── migrations/<timestamp>_add_machine_discovery_catalog/\n+│   │   └── migration.sql\n+│   ├── schema.prisma\n+│   └── seed-machine-discovery.ts\n+├── src/\n+│   ├── app.module.ts\n+│   ├── config/\n+│   │   └── machine-discovery.config.ts\n+│   └── machines/\n+│       ├── machines.module.ts\n+│       ├── presentation/http/\n+│       │   ├── machine-discovery.controller.ts\n+│       │   ├── machine-selection.controller.ts\n+│       │   └── dto/\n+│       ├── application/\n+│       │   ├── discover-machines.service.ts\n+│       │   ├── get-machine-detail.service.ts\n+│       │   ├── resolve-machine-identifier.service.ts\n+│       │   ├── revalidate-machine-selection.service.ts\n+│       │   └── machine-public-view.mapper.ts\n+│       ├── domain/\n+│       │   ├── availability-projector.ts\n+│       │   ├── identifier-normalizer.ts\n+│       │   ├── machine-gateway.port.ts\n+│       │   └── machine.types.ts\n+│       └── infrastructure/\n+│           ├── prisma-machine.repository.ts\n+│           ├── postgres-distance.repository.ts\n+│           ├── signed-catalog-cursor.ts\n+│           └── emulated-machine-gateway.ts\n+└── test/\n+    └── machines/\n+```\n+\n+Controllers validate transport input, invoke one application service, and shape\n+documented responses. They contain no distance, availability, QR, or selection\n+business logic.\n+\n+### Mobile boundaries\n+\n+```text\n+apps/mobile/\n+├── app.config.ts\n+└── src/\n+    ├── app/\n+    │   ├── AppNavigator.tsx\n+    │   ├── AppStore.ts\n+    │   └── navigation/types.ts\n+    ├── features/machine-discovery/\n+    │   ├── api/\n+    │   │   ├── MachineDiscoveryApi.ts\n+    │   │   └── machineDiscovery.contracts.ts\n+    │   ├── native/\n+    │   │   ├── LocationAdapter.ts\n+    │   │   ├── ExpoLocationAdapter.ts\n+    │   │   ├── ScannerAdapter.ts\n+    │   │   └── ExpoScannerAdapter.ts\n+    │   ├── state/\n+    │   │   ├── MachineDiscoveryStore.ts\n+    │   │   ├── MachineDiscoveryWorkflow.ts\n+    │   │   └── machineDiscovery.types.ts\n+    │   ├── screens/\n+    │   │   ├── MachineDiscoveryScreen.tsx\n+    │   │   ├── MachineDetailScreen.tsx\n+    │   │   └── MachineScannerScreen.tsx\n+    │   └── components/\n+    │       ├── MachineMap.tsx\n+    │       ├── MachineList.tsx\n+    │       ├── MachineCard.tsx\n+    │       └── ManualMachineCodeForm.tsx\n+    └── test/\n+        └── machine-discovery/\n+```\n+\n+`MachineDiscoveryStore` is the sole owner of discovery context, deduplicated\n+entities, map/list view, permission, scanner, card, and selected-machine state.\n+React local state is limited to ephemeral visual mechanics that are not shared\n+application state.\n+\n+## Architecture Decisions\n+\n+### Catalog ownership and public identity\n+\n+- PostgreSQL in this application is the only catalog authority.\n+- `machineId` is a cryptographically random, immutable, opaque 128-bit public\n+  identifier encoded as 22 base64url characters. It is distinct from the\n+  internal UUID primary key and from customer-entered codes.\n+- Publication controls public visibility independently from operational status.\n+- Identifier aliases live in a private table and are never serialized.\n+- Development/test data comes from deterministic fixtures, never ERP.\n+\n+### Availability\n+\n+- A backend projector produces only `ONLINE`, `SERVICE`, or `OFFLINE`.\n+- `SERVICE` wins when explicit maintenance/service mode is active.\n+- Otherwise `ONLINE` requires published/non-deleted catalog state, valid price\n+  and currency, fresh authoritative evidence, reachability, readiness, sales\n+  allowance, and no explicit offline evidence.\n+- All missing, unknown, stale, malformed, or conflicting evidence fails closed to\n+  `OFFLINE`. Invalid price/currency also makes selection ineligible.\n+- Detail refresh and selection revalidation query the configured\n+  `MachineGateway` port. The initial implementation binds only the controlled\n+  emulator; no ERP implementation exists or is planned.\n+\n+### Geographic discovery\n+\n+- Coordinates use PostgreSQL `NUMERIC`, with latitude then longitude throughout\n+  all API and domain types.\n+- PostgreSQL calculates Haversine straight-line distance in a parameterized\n+  query. No PostGIS dependency is added for the MVP scale.\n+- Located ordering is `(distanceMeters ASC, machineId ASC)`; non-located ordering\n+  is `(machineId ASC)` within the configured service area.\n+- The opaque signed keyset cursor binds the normalized query context, catalog\n+  ordering revision, last sort key, and expiry. A catalog-ordering revision\n+  mismatch returns a stale-context conflict and requires refresh instead of\n+  silently producing duplicates or omissions.\n+- Optional viewport bounds are validated, bounded, and cursor-bound. Invalid or\n+  missing machine coordinates are excluded from map/distance results but may be\n+  shown in list results without distance when their public address is valid.\n+- User coordinates are request-scoped only and are not persisted or included in\n+  application logs/analytics.\n+\n+### API and stale-result protection\n+\n+- All four routes require the existing access-session guard.\n+- Errors expose stable consumer codes and correlation IDs, never database,\n+  identifier-mapping, telemetry, MQTT, or catalog-internal details.\n+- Unknown, hidden, archived, deleted, non-customer, and inaccessible identifiers\n+  all return the same `MACHINE_NOT_FOUND` representation.\n+- Every public detail carries an opaque `machineRevision` that changes whenever\n+  selection-relevant data changes.\n+- Selection revalidation accepts the displayed revision. If price, publication,\n+  or eligibility changed, it returns `MACHINE_CONTEXT_CHANGED` with a freshly\n+  allowlisted detail and does not produce a handoff.\n+\n+### Mobile state and concurrency\n+\n+- The app-scoped store owns one discovery context across map/list switches.\n+- The workflow uses `switchMap` for superseding position, viewport, refresh,\n+  detail, QR, manual, logout, and account-replacement work; `exhaustMap` for page\n+  append and selection confirmation; and scanner gating plus time-window\n+  suppression for duplicate QR detections.\n+- Each result is tagged with session epoch and request generation. Reducers\n+  ignore results that do not match the active epoch/generation.\n+- Logout/account replacement increments the epoch, cancels subscriptions, clears\n+  catalog/card/scanner/handoff state, and creates a fresh session scope.\n+- Workflow and native adapters with subscriptions expose idempotent `dispose()`.\n+\n+## API Surface\n+\n+| Method | Path | Purpose |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| `GET` | `/api/v1/machines` | Bounded incremental catalog discovery with optional location/viewport context |\n+| `GET` | `/api/v1/machines/{machineId}` | Fresh consumer-safe card/detail |\n+| `POST` | `/api/v1/machines/resolve` | Exact QR or manual-code resolution |\n+| `POST` | `/api/v1/machines/{machineId}/selection-revalidation` | Final current eligibility/price check and typed handoff |\n+\n+The normative transport definition is\n+`contracts/machine-discovery.openapi.yaml`. The navigation-only handoff is\n+defined in `contracts/selection-handoff.md`.\n+\n+## Schema Changes and Forward Migrations\n+\n+One forward migration, `add_machine_discovery_catalog`, introduces:\n+\n+1. Enums for publication state, service mode, evidence state, and identifier\n+   kind.\n+2. `machines` with separate internal UUID and immutable unique public identity.\n+3. `machine_locations` with address and paired validated coordinates.\n+4. `machine_prices` with exact PostgreSQL numeric amount, ISO currency, and\n+   selection revision fields.\n+5. `machine_identifier_mappings` with normalized exact aliases and global\n+   collision protection.\n+6. `machine_availability_evidence` with freshness timestamps and emulator-owned\n+   readiness facts.\n+7. `machine_catalog_state` singleton with ordering revision used by cursors.\n+8. Check constraints, foreign keys, partial indexes, and exact-lookup indexes\n+   listed in `data-model.md`.\n+\n+No automatic schema synchronization is allowed. The migration is forward-only;\n+rollback in production is restore/forward-fix, not destructive down migration.\n+Seed data is separate from the migration and is allowed only in controlled\n+development/test environments.\n+\n+## New Runtime Dependencies\n+\n+| Package | Scope | Reason |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| `expo-location` | mobile | Foreground permission and one-shot/coarse-to-precise position behind a typed adapter |\n+| `expo-camera` | mobile | `CameraView` barcode scanning restricted to configured QR types; no media recording |\n+| `react-native-maps` | mobile | Stable SDK 54-supported native map; avoids the alpha `expo-maps` package |\n+\n+Use `pnpm --filter @kaynann/mobile exec expo install <package>` so Expo selects\n+the SDK-compatible release. SDK 54 documentation currently lists\n+`expo-location ~19.0.8`, `expo-camera ~17.0.10`, and `react-native-maps 1.20.1`;\n+the lockfile, not a hand-entered version, is authoritative at implementation\n+time. No backend runtime dependency and no geospatial extension are required.\n+\n+## Native Configuration\n+\n+- Add Expo config plugins for foreground location and camera permission strings.\n+- Do not request background location or microphone/recording permission.\n+- Configure Android Google Maps API key through deployment secrets/config, never\n+  source control. iOS defaults to Apple Maps unless product requirements later\n+  approve Google Maps configuration.\n+- Model `undetermined`, `granted`, `denied`, `restricted`, `revoked`, and\n+  `unavailable` states. All non-granted states retain list/manual fallback.\n+- Mount only one camera preview and disable/unmount it whenever the scanner route\n+  is unfocused.\n+\n+## Delivery Phases\n+\n+### Phase 0: Research\n+\n+Complete `research.md`: ownership, identifiers/QR, exact mappings,\n+availability/freshness, money, geography/pagination, native packages, safe\n+errors, MobX/RxJS teardown, and emulator decisions. Unknown production protocol\n+facts become fail-closed rollout gates, not implementation clarifications.\n+\n+### Phase 1: Design and contracts\n+\n+Complete `data-model.md`, OpenAPI, typed selection handoff, and `quickstart.md`.\n+Validate the plan against the constitution and refresh the managed Spec Kit\n+agent context.\n+\n+### Phase 2: Implementation outline\n+\n+1. Add the reviewed Prisma migration, configuration schema, fixtures, and\n+   deterministic clock.\n+2. Implement domain normalization, availability projection, emulator port, and\n+   repositories with unit/integration tests.\n+3. Implement authenticated controllers, DTO shaping, OpenAPI generation, and\n+   security/pagination contract tests.\n+4. Add Expo dependencies/config and typed API/native adapters.\n+5. Implement the MobX store and RxJS workflow with cancellation/session tests.\n+6. Build map/list, card, scanner, and manual fallback following mockups without\n+   their illustrative data or out-of-scope controls.\n+7. Run backend, mobile, permission, concurrency, stale-result, and E2E scenarios\n+   from `quickstart.md`.\n+\n+This outline informs later task generation; this command does not modify\n+`tasks.md` or production code.\n+\n+## Testing Strategy\n+\n+- Domain unit tests: normalization, alias collisions, status precedence,\n+  freshness boundaries, price validation, QR grammar, cursor signatures.\n+- PostgreSQL integration tests: constraints, Haversine ordering, tie-breakers,\n+  viewport bounds, revision conflicts, safe lookup, and pagination completeness.\n+- API tests: authentication, validation, allowlist snapshots, uniform not-found,\n+  stale selection, retry-safe errors, and absence of sensitive fields.\n+- Mobile tests: store ownership, deduplication, map/list continuity, permissions,\n+  scanner/manual fallback, request supersession, duplicate event suppression,\n+  session epoch teardown, and `dispose()`.\n+- E2E: deterministic catalog, map/list/card/scanner flow, price/status changes,\n+  delayed response races, logout/account replacement, and no purchase side\n+  effects.\n+\n+## Complexity and Risk Register\n+\n+| Risk | Mitigation / gate |\n+|---|---|\n+| Production machine protocol is unconfirmed | Emulator only; production adapter cannot ship until authenticated protocol, payload semantics, freshness, and failure modes are approved. |\n+| Legacy QR payload is unverified | Development-only fixtures; production legacy format remains disabled until an anonymized real payload is supplied and validated. |\n+| Catalog mutates during pagination | Cursor binds catalog ordering revision; stale contexts fail explicitly and refresh. |\n+| Native permission/platform differences | Typed adapters, exhaustive permission states, device tests, list/manual fallback. |\n+| Price/status changes between card and confirmation | Selection endpoint refreshes and compares `machineRevision`; changed context never advances. |\n+| Sensitive internal data leaks | DTO allowlist tests and a uniform external error taxonomy. |\n+| Future growth exceeds numeric Haversine query | Measure query plans and catalog size first; PostGIS is a later evidence-based migration, not an MVP dependency. |\n+\n+## Explicitly Out of Scope\n+\n+- Volume selection, total calculation, purchase, reservation, payment, wallet,\n+  dispensing sessions, or machine commands\n+- MQTT command workflows, firmware, or production machine connectivity\n+- Payment-provider merchant QR\n+- Search, favorites, ratings, routes, booking, advanced filters, or catalog admin\n+- Any current or future ERP integration or indirect/direct runtime dependency\n+- ERP adapters, clients, ingestion ports, synchronization, import, polling,\n+  webhooks, shared databases, code/DTO ports, or ERP-derived seed data\n+\n*** Add File: specs/006-machine-discovery-and-selection/research.md\n+# Phase 0 Research: Machine Discovery and Selection\n+\n+## 1. Independent Consumer Catalog Ownership\n+\n+**Decision**: The new NestJS/PostgreSQL application exclusively owns the\n+consumer machine catalog, public identity, publication state, address,\n+coordinates, price, identifier mappings, and availability evidence. Controlled\n+fixtures seed development/test data; production catalog management is a separate\n+future operational concern.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumer contracts require stable public semantics and strict\n+privacy boundaries that the legacy implementation does not provide. Local\n+ownership makes availability, errors, migrations, and response shaping explicit\n+and testable.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- ERP master/read-model synchronization: rejected. No current or future ERP\n+  integration exists or is planned.\n+- Runtime ERP API/database access: rejected for ownership, reliability,\n+  security, and explicit product-scope reasons.\n+- Static mobile catalog: rejected because publication, price, availability, and\n+  exact resolution must be authoritative at request time.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Define a non-ERP operational process for creating\n+and maintaining catalog records before production. This does not block the\n+fixture/emulator implementation.\n+\n+## 2. Public Identifier and Versioned QR Contract\n+\n+**Decision**: Give each machine an immutable random 128-bit `machineId`, encoded\n+as 22 base64url characters and distinct from the internal UUID. Support only the\n+trusted application URI form `kaynann://machine/v1/<machineId>` for v1 QR plus\n+explicitly registered legacy formats. QR parsing returns a candidate identifier;\n+the backend still performs exact authoritative resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Random public identity is stable, opaque, non-sequential, and does\n+not disclose serial/modem/database values. A versioned grammar permits controlled\n+evolution and rejects arbitrary URLs and payment-provider QR payloads.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- Expose database UUID or serial/modem ID: rejected because it couples public\n+  contracts to internal identity.\n+- Accept any URL/deep link: rejected because it enables unsafe redirects and\n+  ambiguous interpretation.\n+- Put price/status in QR: rejected because QR content is not authoritative and\n+  becomes stale.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Approve the final trusted application address and\n+key-rotation/deep-link ownership configuration. Production legacy compatibility\n+remains disabled until the customer supplies an anonymized real payload and it\n+passes grammar, uniqueness, and security review.\n+\n+## 3. Current and Legacy Identifier Normalization\n+\n+**Decision**: Store aliases in a private normalized mapping table with a global\n+unique constraint on `(kind, normalizedValue)`. Manual current/legacy codes are\n+trimmed, ASCII-only, uppercased with locale-independent rules, then validated by\n+the scheme-specific grammar. QR values are parsed structurally and never passed\n+through broad case folding. Resolution is exact; zero and multiple matches both\n+fail safely.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Scheme-specific canonicalization avoids fuzzy/accidental matches.\n+Database uniqueness makes the one-alias-to-one-machine invariant enforceable\n+under concurrency.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- Fuzzy/substring lookup: rejected as unsafe for physical machine selection.\n+- Case-insensitive database search over raw input: rejected because normalization\n+  rules differ by scheme and can create hidden collisions.\n+- Copy legacy DTOs/internal IDs: rejected because mappings are private data owned\n+  by this application.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Register every production scheme with grammar,\n+normalization examples, ownership, and collision tests. Unknown schemes fail\n+closed.\n+\n+## 4. Availability Projection and Freshness\n+\n+**Decision**: Project customer state server-side. Explicit service/maintenance\n+mode yields `SERVICE`. Otherwise `ONLINE` requires fresh authoritative evidence,\n+reachable/ready/sales-allowed facts, no explicit offline state, valid current\n+price/currency, and public catalog eligibility. Every other case is `OFFLINE`.\n+Freshness uses an injected clock and configured maximum age.\n+\n+**Rationale**: A strict fail-closed projection prevents mobile from inferring\n+machine health and makes stale/missing evidence safe. An injected clock makes\n+boundary behavior deterministic.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- Trust a single connected flag: rejected because connected is not equivalent to\n+  customer readiness.\n+- Let mobile interpret raw telemetry: rejected because it leaks internals and\n+  creates inconsistent clients.\n+- Treat missing evidence as online: rejected as unsafe.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: The production gateway remains disabled until the\n+customer confirms authenticated protocol, authoritative fields, freshness SLA,\n+clock semantics, state precedence, and disconnect behavior. Until then only the\n+application-owned emulator is valid.\n+\n+## 5. Exact Money Representation\n+\n+**Decision**: Store price per liter as PostgreSQL `NUMERIC(20,6)` through Prisma\n+`Decimal`, validate it as positive and within configured bounds, and serialize it\n+as a canonical decimal string with an uppercase three-letter ISO 4217 currency.\n+Never convert it to a JavaScript floating-point number for business comparison.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Decimal storage supports exact per-liter prices, including\n+currencies/price precision not safely represented by binary floating point. A\n+string transport preserves exactness for clients.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- JavaScript/PostgreSQL floating point: rejected due to rounding ambiguity.\n+- Integer minor units only: viable for charged totals but less suitable for a\n+  per-liter rate whose scale may exceed the currency's payment minor unit.\n+- Currency inferred from location: rejected; price currency is authoritative.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Confirm supported currencies and commercial price\n+bounds before production catalog entry. Unknown/invalid currency fails closed.\n+\n+## 6. Coordinates, Distance, Viewport, and Pagination\n+\n+**Decision**: Validate latitude in `[-90, 90]`, longitude in `[-180, 180]`, and\n+require both or neither. Store as `NUMERIC(9,6)`. Use a parameterized PostgreSQL\n+Haversine expression for straight-line distance. Located sort order is exact\n+computed distance then public `machineId`; unlocated sort is `machineId`.\n+Use signed keyset cursors bound to query context and catalog ordering revision.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Haversine is adequate for display/ranking at MVP regional scale,\n+uses current infrastructure, and avoids an unjustified extension. Revision-bound\n+cursors return a clear stale-context conflict rather than silently paging across\n+changed ordering.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- PostGIS/geography: technically stronger for large radius/polygon queries but\n+  rejected until scale/query-plan evidence justifies infrastructure and migration\n+  cost.\n+- Client distance/sort: rejected because clients would receive a non-authoritative\n+  subset and produce inconsistent pagination.\n+- Offset pagination: rejected because concurrent publication/location changes can\n+  duplicate or omit entries.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Load-test representative catalog density and inspect\n+query plans. Adopt PostGIS only if measured latency/index behavior misses the\n+feature target. Validate the configured service-area rectangle for production;\n+antimeridian-spanning areas require a reviewed split representation.\n+\n+## 7. Map, Location, Camera, and QR Packages\n+\n+**Decision**:\n+\n+- `react-native-maps` for map rendering.\n+- `expo-location` for foreground permission/current location only.\n+- `expo-camera` `CameraView` for QR detection, restricted to QR barcode type.\n+- Manual code input is always available when camera/location is denied,\n+  restricted, revoked, unavailable, or temporarily failing.\n+\n+Install via `expo install` for pinned SDK compatibility. Expo SDK 54 documentation\n+lists `react-native-maps 1.20.1`, `expo-location ~19.0.8`, and\n+`expo-camera ~17.0.10`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: These are the minimal Expo-supported native dependencies.\n+`react-native-maps` is stable for SDK 54, while SDK 54 `expo-maps` is alpha and\n+requires development builds. Existing MobX/RxJS packages cover state/workflows.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- `expo-maps`: rejected for this feature because the official SDK 54 module is\n+  alpha and carries breaking-change risk.\n+- Third-party QR parser/scanner: rejected; `expo-camera` already exposes barcode\n+  results and application code owns the strict URI grammar.\n+- Background location: rejected because discovery only needs user-initiated\n+  foreground position and must minimize collection.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Device-test iOS/Android permissions, scanner focus,\n+camera denial, Android Maps API key injection, and store disclosure strings.\n+\n+**Official references**:\n+\n+- https://docs.expo.dev/versions/v54.0.0/sdk/map-view/\n+- https://docs.expo.dev/versions/v54.0.0/sdk/location/\n+- https://docs.expo.dev/versions/v54.0.0/sdk/camera/\n+\n+## 8. Safe API Error Taxonomy\n+\n+**Decision**: Return a stable problem envelope with consumer code, safe message,\n+HTTP status, retryability, and correlation ID. Use:\n+\n+- `AUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED` / `SESSION_INVALID` (`401`)\n+- `VALIDATION_ERROR` / `MACHINE_CODE_INVALID` (`400`)\n+- `MACHINE_NOT_FOUND` (`404`) for every absent/inaccessible category\n+- `DISCOVERY_CONTEXT_STALE` / `MACHINE_CONTEXT_CHANGED` (`409`)\n+- `MACHINE_NOT_SELECTABLE` (`422`)\n+- `DISCOVERY_TEMPORARILY_UNAVAILABLE` (`503`, retryable)\n+\n+**Rationale**: This supports deterministic UX without exposing whether a hidden,\n+archived, deleted, legacy-mapped, or internal machine exists.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- Raw framework/database errors: rejected due to disclosure and instability.\n+- Different errors for hidden/deleted/unknown: rejected due to enumeration risk.\n+- Treat connectivity failure as not-found: rejected because clients need safe\n+  retry behavior.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Verify gateway timeout/retry mapping once the\n+production protocol is approved; do not expose upstream details.\n+\n+## 9. MobX/RxJS Cancellation and Session Cleanup\n+\n+**Decision**: One MobX store owns all long-lived discovery state. An app-scoped\n+workflow accepts intents and composes typed Observable-first API/native adapters.\n+Use `switchMap` for supersedable work, `exhaustMap` for pagination and selection,\n+and a session epoch plus request generation on every result. `takeUntil` scopes\n+work to logout/account replacement/disposal; finalizers clear loading gates.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Operator semantics make race behavior explicit. Epoch/generation\n+checks are a second barrier when an underlying native/network operation cannot be\n+physically cancelled.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- Independent React state and effects per screen: rejected because map/list/card\n+  continuity and session teardown become nondeterministic.\n+- Public Subjects in screens: rejected because lifecycle and ownership leak into\n+  presentation code.\n+- Promise-only adapters: rejected because cancellation/supersession becomes\n+  implicit and inconsistent.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: None. Race, delayed-response, logout, replacement,\n+and idempotent `dispose()` behavior must pass automated tests before merge.\n+\n+## 10. Test Catalog and Availability Emulator\n+\n+**Decision**: Provide deterministic, non-production seed/fixture data owned by\n+this repository plus an emulator implementing the `MachineGateway` port. The\n+emulator can change evidence, price, publication, and delays and uses an injected\n+test clock for freshness edges.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Development and automated tests need realistic state transitions\n+before a production machine protocol exists, without creating an accidental ERP\n+dependency.\n+\n+**Alternatives considered**:\n+\n+- ERP import/synchronization/adapter: rejected, including as a temporary option.\n+- Hardcoded mobile-only fixtures: rejected because they bypass API, persistence,\n+  security, and freshness behavior.\n+- Assume a future MQTT payload now: rejected because unconfirmed semantics would\n+  become accidental production architecture.\n+\n+**Production rollout gates**: Production configuration must reject emulator mode.\n+A future production gateway requires a separately approved protocol plan. It must\n+not be ERP-based.\n+\n*** End Patch";
text(await tools.apply_patch(patch));
