ll UI tasks, T118 depends on backend tasks, T119 depends on mobile tasks, T120 depends on T118, T121-T122 depend on T119, and T123 depends on T118-T122.\n@@\n-- **US1 (P1)**: Depends only on Foundational. It owns catalog discovery and map/list presentation.\n-- **US2 (P1)**: Depends only on Foundational. It resolves fixtures directly and does not require US1 discovery.\n-- **US3 (P1)**: Depends only on Foundational. Its independent test opens a fixture by known public `machineId`; navigation integration may reuse US1/US2 entry points after both exist.\n-- **US4 (P2)**: Depends only on Foundational for its independent delayed/stale harness. For release hardening, apply it after US1-US3 so all four routes and three screens receive the protections.\n-- **US5 (P2)**: Depends only on Foundational and mocked flows for independent session-isolation testing. For release, apply it after active US1-US3 workflows exist.\n+- **US1 (P1)**: Depends only on Foundational. It owns catalog discovery, map/list presentation, and the shared refreshed read-only machine card/detail route.\n+- **US2 (P1)**: Depends on US1's card/detail route but not its discovery results; its independent test enters through QR/manual fixtures and verifies the resolved card directly.\n+- **US3 (P1)**: Depends on US1's read-only detail increment and extends it with final revalidation, confirmation, and typed handoff. It does not require US2 identification to run its known-public-ID independent test.\n+- **US4 (P2)**: Depends on US1-US3 because its cross-route allowlist and stale-response assertions exercise all four API routes and every P1 mobile workflow.\n+- **US5 (P2)**: Depends on US1-US3 because its session-isolation test cancels the actual discovery, detail, QR/manual, and confirmation workflows rather than placeholders.\n@@\n-- **US1**: T041-T046 are written and observed failing first; T047 depends on T041-T042; T048 depends on T043 and T047; T049-T050 depend on T048; T051 depends on T045; T052 depends on T037; T053 depends on T046 and T035; T054 precedes UI; T055-T056 depend on T054; T057 precedes T058; T059 follows T058; T060 follows the complete story.\n-- **US2**: T061-T064 fail first; T065-T066 satisfy T061; T067-T068 satisfy T062; T069 satisfies T063; T070 satisfies T064; T071 precedes UI; T072 and T073 precede T074; T075 follows T074; T076 follows the complete story.\n-- **US3**: T077-T080 fail first; T081 precedes T082-T083; T084-T085 follow services; T086-T087 satisfy the mobile flow test; T088 precedes UI; T089 precedes T090; T091 follows handoff implementation; T092 follows the complete story.\n+- **US1**: T041-T046 are written and observed failing first; T047 depends on T041-T042; T048 depends on T043 and T047; T049-T050 depend on T044 and T048; T051 depends on T045; T052 depends on T037; T053 depends on T035, T046, and T050; T054 precedes UI; T055-T056 depend on T054; T057 precedes T058; T059 follows T058; T060 follows the complete story.\n+- **US2**: US1 completes first; T061-T064 then fail first; T065-T066 satisfy T061; T067-T068 satisfy T062; T069 satisfies T063; T070 satisfies T064 and depends on the US1 detail route; T071 precedes UI; T072 and T073 precede T074; T075 follows T074; T076 follows the complete story.\n+- **US3**: US1 completes first; T077-T080 then fail first; T081 precedes T082-T083; T084-T085 follow services and T085 completes module wiring; T086-T087 satisfy the mobile flow test; T088 precedes UI; T089 precedes T090; T091 follows handoff implementation; T092 follows the complete story.\n@@\n-- After T012, foundational domain test/implementation chains T015-T026 can be split by file; mobile chains T034-T040 can proceed alongside backend infrastructure T027-T033.\n-- After Foundational, US1-US5 can start in parallel because each has fixture/fake-backed independent tests; integrate shared files such as `machine-discovery-flow.service.ts`, controllers, repository, and navigator sequentially in Task ID order to avoid conflicts.\n+- After T012, foundational domain test/implementation chains T015-T026 can be split by file; mobile chains T034-T040 can proceed alongside backend infrastructure T027-T033.\n+- After Foundational, US1 starts first. Once US1 is complete, US2 and US3 may proceed in parallel where their `[P]` tasks and files do not conflict; US4 and US5 start only after all P1 workflows exist.\n@@\n Parallel batch A: T041, T042, T043, T044, T045, T046\n After backend tests exist: T047 -> T048 -> T049 -> T050\n-After mockup notes T054: T055 and T056 in parallel, then T057 -> T058 -> T059 -> T060\n+After API/detail and mockup notes: T053; T055 and T056 in parallel, then T057 -> T058 -> T059 -> T060\n@@\n-Parallel batch A: T061, T062, T063, T064\n+After US1: parallel batch A: T061, T062, T063, T064\n@@\n-Parallel batch A: T077, T078, T079, T080\n+After US1: parallel batch A: T077, T078, T079, T080\n@@\n-Parallel batch A: T093, T094, T095, T096\n+After US1-US3: parallel batch A: T093, T094, T095, T096\n@@\n-Parallel batch A: T104, T105, T106\n+After US1-US3: parallel batch A: T104, T105, T106\n@@\n-3. Deliver the three P1 stories in order for a single-team path: discovery, exact identification, then fresh detail/selection. Each remains fixture/emulator-backed and independently demonstrable.\n+3. Deliver US1 first because it establishes discovery and the shared read-only card. Then implement US2 identification and US3 selection; each remains fixture/emulator-backed and independently demonstrable after its declared prerequisites.\n*** End Patch";
